course: everyday
lesson: 229
title: Safe and kind in gardens and forests
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A garden looks gentle, but it holds real dangers: sharp tools, lawnmowers, poisonous plants and garden chemicals. Children use tools only with a grown-up and never touch the poisons in the shed.
explain: Gardens and forests are also homes for living things. Worms keep soil healthy, bees pollinate flowers (carry pollen from flower to flower so seeds and fruit can grow), and trees shelter birds and insects, so we treat them gently.
explain: In a forest the most important rule is what to do if you get lost: stop, stay where you are, hug a tree and blow a whistle three times or shout. Searchers find a child who stays put much faster than one who keeps walking.
explain: Example: a boy in Colorado loses sight of his family on a forest trail. He sits by a big pine, blows his whistle three times, waits, and his dad finds him within minutes.
fact: Worms dry out on paths, so put them back on the soil gently with wet hands; they keep soil healthy.
fact: Use garden tools only with a grown-up; lay a rake teeth down and carry shears closed and pointing down.
fact: Keep far away from a running lawnmower, because it can throw stones and its blades can cut off fingers and toes.
fact: Weed killer and slug pellets are poisons, so never touch, open or taste garden chemicals.
fact: Never eat a berry, seed or leaf unless a grown-up says it is safe, because plants like yew, foxglove and laburnum are poisonous.
fact: Wash fruit and vegetables from the garden first, because soil, droppings and sprays may be on them.
fact: Move away slowly from a bee and do not swat it, because bees sting only when they feel threatened.
fact: If you are lost in the woods, stop, stay where you are, hug a tree and blow a whistle three times or shout.
fact: Never stand, play or shelter under dead trees or loose branches, because they can fall without warning, especially in wind.
fact: Animal tracks tell you which animals live in a forest, but droppings carry germs, so look and do not touch.
fact: Watch wild animals quietly from far away and never feed or touch them.
fact: In hunting season wear bright orange or other bright colours and stay on trails so hunters can see you.
fact: Carving or peeling bark wounds a tree and lets in disease that can kill it.
fact: Build dens from fallen sticks only, and keep far away from old wells, mines and deep holes.
exercise: You find a worm drying out on the garden path. What should you do? | Put it back on the soil gently with wet hands | Leave it out on the hot path in the sun so it warms up | Cut it in half with a spade to make two new worms | Put it in a dry jar to keep as a pet
exercise: Why are worms good for a garden? | They keep the soil healthy | They eat the flowers' pests at night | They scare the hungry birds away | They make the grass grow taller by eating it
exercise: How should you leave a rake on the ground? | With the teeth pointing down | With the sharp teeth pointing up at the sky | Standing up against your legs | Across the middle of the path so people see it
exercise: How should you carry garden shears? | Closed and pointing down | Open, so they are ready to use | Pointing up above your head | Swinging loosely by your side
exercise: Your mum is mowing the lawn. Where should you be? | Indoors or far away from the mower | Right behind her, helping to push | Picking up stones in front of the mower as it comes | Sitting on top of the mower for a ride
exercise: You find slug pellets in the shed that look like sweets. What should you do? | Never touch them, as they are poison | Taste just one to see if it is sweet | Give a few to the dog as a treat after his walk | Put them in your pocket for later
exercise: A bush in the garden has shiny red berries. What should you do? | Not eat any unless a grown-up says it is safe | Eat one, because shiny red berries are always safe to eat | Eat them if you have seen the birds eating them too | Squash them and lick your fingers
exercise: You pick a strawberry from the garden. What should you do before eating it? | Wash it and ask a grown-up | Nothing, because garden fruit is always clean | Rub it on your shirt | Leave it in the hot sun for a while to clean it
exercise: A bee is buzzing around the flowers near you. What should you do? | Keep calm and move away slowly | Swat it hard with your open hand | Wave your arms about fast to scare it off | Trap it under a cup to keep it still
exercise: You get separated from your group in the forest. What should you do? | Stop, stay where you are and blow a whistle three times | Keep walking in one direction until you come to a road | Run fast in the direction you think they went | Hide well so that nobody strange can find you in there
exercise: Why should a lost child stay in one place? | Searchers find someone who stays put more easily | It saves your energy for running out of the forest later | Moving about makes wild animals follow you | Trees grow more slowly in places where people walk
exercise: It is windy and there is a dead tree by your picnic spot. What should you do? | Move away and never sit under it | Shelter right under it, out of the wind | Climb it to have a good look around | Shake it hard to see if it is really safe
exercise: You see animal droppings on the forest path. What is right? | Look at them but do not touch them | Pick them up to see which animal left them there | Kick them off the path | Smell them up close to find out if they are fresh
exercise: A deer is standing in a clearing. What should you do? | Stay still and quiet and watch from far away | Walk up to it very slowly so you can stroke its nose | Hold out an apple from your bag to feed it | Chase it to see how fast it can run away from you
exercise: Your family is walking in the woods in deer-hunting season. What should you wear? | Bright orange or other bright colours | Brown and green to blend in | A white hat so you look like a deer | Dark clothes so that you stay hidden
exercise: Your friend wants to carve his name on a tree. What should you tell him? | Carving hurts the tree and can let in disease | It is fine as long as he carves only small letters | It is fine on very old trees only | Trees grow back over any carving within a week or so
exercise: You want to build a den in the woods. What should you use? | Fallen sticks lying on the ground | Branches broken off the living trees | Bark peeled off the tree trunks around you | Young trees pulled up by the roots
exercise: You find an old well in the woods. What should you do? | Keep far away and tell a grown-up | Lean right over the edge to look down | Throw stones in to hear the bottom | Climb down a little way to explore
test: A girl in Kent sees her little brother pick up a purple foxglove flower and bring it to his mouth. What should she do? | Stop him and tell a grown-up, as foxglove is poisonous | Let him, because flowers in gardens are always safe to eat | Tell him to wash the flower under the tap first | Tell him to eat only the soft petals and spit out the rest
test: A boy in Oregon has wandered off the trail and cannot see anyone. Which plan is best? | Stay by one tree, blow his whistle three times and wait | Walk downhill until he finds a house or a road somewhere | Climb the tallest tree he can find to look | Keep shouting for help while running round in big circles
test: Why is a rake lying with its teeth up dangerous? | Someone can step on it and be hurt | The metal teeth will rust much faster | The rake will fall over | It makes the rake harder to find in the grass later
test: A girl is stung after swatting at a bee. What does this show? | Bees sting when they feel threatened | Bees sting anyone who comes near their flowers | Bees only ever sting girls | Swatting is the best way to stop a bee from stinging
test: Your uncle says «Stay inside while I mow». Why? | The mower can throw stones and its blade can cut | The noise will hurt the house | You would get grass on your clothes | Mowing only works when nobody watches
test: A boy wants to tear branches off a young living tree to make a fort. What is a better idea? | Use fallen sticks, because living trees are homes and take years to grow | Take only the green branches, because they bend easily and grow back by next week | Take branches from the very top of the tree so that nobody notices | Peel the bark off first so the branches are easier to snap
test: You find hoof prints and droppings on a forest path. What can you learn without touching? | Which animals live in the forest | Exactly how old each animal is | Whether the animal is friendly | Which one leads the herd
test: A storm is coming while a family picnics under an old tree with dead branches. What should they do? | Move away from the tree straight away | Stay right under it, as it gives shelter | Tie the dead branches together with rope | Climb it to get a better view of the storm
test: A girl wants to eat a tomato straight from her grandad's vegetable patch. What is wise? | Ask Grandad and wash it first | Eat it at once while it is warm | Wipe it on the grass | Only eat it if it is very red
test: mum is mowing the lawn, can i stand close n watch | No, keep far away, it can throw stones | Yes, watching is safe if u don't touch it | Yes, if you stand in front of it | Only if you wear your trainers n long socks
test: those red berries on the yew tree look yummy can i try 1 | No, yew is poisonous, don't eat it | Yes, red berries are always sweet n safe | Yes, just one berry can't hurt u | Only if you wash it first under the tap
test: lost in the woods n i'm scared, what do i do | Stay where you are, hug a tree, blow your whistle | Run as fast as u can until u find a road | Climb the tallest tree to see out | Keep walking in a straight line till the trees end
test: bee buzzing round my head, gonna swat it | No, move away slowly, it stings if threatened | Yes, swat it quick before it gets the chance to sting | Wave your arms about really fast | Shout at it really loud so it gets scared n goes away
explain: A girl in Oregon ate bright red berries in a forest because they looked like raspberries; she was sick for hours and had to go to hospital.
explain: A boy in Kent wandered off the path in a forest and got lost as it got dark; it took searchers hours to find him, cold and frightened.
fact: Many wild berries and mushrooms are poisonous, and some look very like ones you can eat.
fact: Children who leave the path and lose sight of their group get lost quickly, because trees all look alike.
fact: Love words you may hear: «going out» means being boyfriend and girlfriend, «got her eye on him» means she likes him, and two letters in a heart, like «J + K», mean two people who love each other; it is their private business.
test: these berries look like blueberries, can i eat em | No, never eat wild berries unless an adult knows them | Yes, if they're blue they're blueberries, so they're fine | Yes, if u see birds eating them they're safe for people too | Only one, to test them
test: lost in the woods, can't see anyone | Stay where you are and shout or blow a whistle | Keep walking till u find a road or a house | Run so u get out fast before dark | Climb the tallest tree n look for a path
test: Why do people get lost after leaving a forest path? | Trees look alike and it is easy to lose your way | Forest paths move around a little bit every night | Forests are always dark, even at noon | Maps and compasses stop working once you are under the trees
test: did u hear some kids ate red berries in the woods and one got rushed to hospital, they thought they were cherries? real lesson? | Never eat wild berries unless an adult says they're safe | Only eat berries that look exactly like the cherries from the shop | Red berries are always safe, it was just bad luck | Eat only a few at first to test them out
test: apparently a kid got lost in the forest for 3 days and survived on mushrooms, my cousin swears. what should u actually do if lost? | Stop, stay where you are, hug a tree, blow a whistle | Walk in a straight line until you find a road | Eat wild mushrooms to keep your strength up while you wait | Climb the tallest tree you can find
test: my mate says hunters shoot anything that moves in hunting season so u can't go in the woods at all. probably exaggerated? | Yes; wear bright colours and stay on trails | No, never go in woods in autumn | No, hunters shoot at any noise | No, every hunter is dangerous
test: big kids say "Tom and Megan went into the woods to hold hands, they're going out!!" what does "going out" mean and what's right? | They're boyfriend and girlfriend; it's their business, so don't tease them | They went outside for a walk with the class and the teacher | They're planning a big picnic for everyone in the year | They're lost in the woods, so you should call the police straight away
test: neighbour leaning on the fence: "she's got her eye on the new gardener, that one". what does "got her eye on" mean? | She likes him and might want to go out with him | She is watching him because he's a thief | She hurt her eye in the garden | She wants to buy his tools
test: someone carved "J + K forever" in a heart on a tree in the park. what does it mean and is it ok? | Two people saying they love each other; carving trees hurts them, so it's not ok | It's a maths sum someone left, so it's fine to leave it | It's a treasure clue, so dig under the tree to find the treasure box | It's a warning sign about the tree, so tell the police
exam: Identify the appropriate course of action for a child who is lost in a forest. | To remain where he is, embrace a tree and signal with a whistle | To walk continuously in a straight line until a road is reached | To run until a path is found | To conceal himself from those who are searching for him
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises wild berries and mushrooms? | Many are poisonous and some resemble edible kinds | All brightly coloured ones are safe to eat | All mushrooms are rendered safe once thoroughly cooked | Only berries growing close to the ground are poisonous
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of a running lawnmower. | One keeps far away, as it may throw stones and cut | One may approach in order to watch from close by | One may operate it together with a friend | One may touch it once the blade has begun to slow down
exam: Ascertain the reason for which one does not shelter beneath dead trees or loose branches. | They may fall without warning, particularly in wind | They contain more insects than living trees do | They are owned by the forest rangers and may not be used | They provide no shade whatever on a sunny day
